From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 10:50:39 -0400
Subject: keep folders across restarts, and stop a window with folders crashing
 on close

Persistence first. Every tab carries its folder ancestry -- ids, titles,
collapse state, outermost first -- in per-tab session extra data, rewritten by
a per-window FolderSessionService whenever the model changes. After session
restore the parked paths are rebuilt level by level through the same
TabStripModel::AddToNewFolder the context menu uses, so restore exercises no
special machinery, and the nested case is covered by a unit test that parks
hand-written paths and checks the tree comes back folder-in-folder, collapse
state included.

The crash was found by that test's fixture, not by any screenshot: closing the
last tab of a collection detaches the collection and then pulls the tab out of
it. That order only works for groups, which are parked alive in
detached_group_collections_; a folder has no park, so the detach freed the
folder while it still owned the tab, and the next call landed on freed memory.
Every window with a folder would have crashed on close. Folders now give up
their tab first and the emptied folder chain is peeled afterwards.

The service also removes itself as a tab strip observer on destruction, which
the fixture teardown demanded and the real teardown deserves anyway.

Why: folders that vanish on restart are an arrangement the browser forgets,
and a close-crash is data loss.
Removable when: the session service grows first-class collection persistence.
